  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124474,
      "id": "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d",
      "title": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "fb3a2cf4-a763-51b4-8968-058f703a2716",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=4-2x,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$\n- **II.** $x\\in (-4,\\infty)$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-6,-2)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $-2$ and $y$-intercept $4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124474/plots/67e787d70f5b0e34fd4372b3793458353390eb80201ffe8399a0dd236cfad7b7-0.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty),$ it is also invertible on $x\\in (-4,\\infty)$ and $x\\in (-6,-2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "536a4b05-af2e-5291-a6f9-85c3d81bd462",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
