      ":question/id": "71b9477a-9fb8-5a20-bc1c-a35e9ca9a3b2",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=2x+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$\n- **II.** $x\\in (-1,\\infty)$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-10,5)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $2$ and $y$-intercept $2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124474/plots/034726ac6f0cd768a28eebbe0bd1d0e43f3d802f92c5902423d9de21410322b4-0.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (-1,\\infty)$ and $x\\in (-10,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\".",
